  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Clarifying Key Access**\n\nI\'m focusing on the user\'s question about the three keys. I\'ve confirmed they\'re discussing Changelly API keys: public, private, and base74. My primary goal is to determine the appropriate roles for each key and access limitations. I\'m moving toward creating a clear explanation to answer the user\'s access question.\n\n\n"
    modified_response: "### API Key Configuration & Security Overview\n\nTo connect your **CryptoSwap Elite** applet seamlessly with the Changelly API, you will configure the standard pair of keys. Here is how they map and who can see them:\n\n---\n\n### 1. Which Keys are Configured?\nYou will use two of the keys you generated:\n*   **`CHANGELLY_API_KEY`**: Provide your **Public API Key** (standard string format).\n*   **`CHANGELLY_API_SECRET`**: Provide your **Private API Secret Key** (used to generate cryptographic HMAC-SHA512 signatures on your backend).\n\n*(Note: The \"Base64\" version of the Public Key is only used if explicitly requested by Changelly during registration, which you can provide to them via email, but it is not needed here.)*\n\n---\n\n### 2. Who Can See These Keys?\nWe built your applet with a secure **Full-Stack (Server + Client) architecture** to guarantee absolute API key protection:\n\n*   **Completely Invisible to Customers**: Your API keys are strictly loaded and executed on the secure, server-side Express runtime. They are **never** exposed to the browser, the frontend logs, or network inspectors.\n*   **Administrator Limits**: Only you (the owner of the AI Studio workspace) and the secure runtime environment can read the values configured within the **Secrets panel** of the AI Studio settings. \n\nThis ensures your keys remain entirely safe from external abuse, spoofing, or unauthorized extraction while providing complete access to Changelly payments."
    thinking_duration {
      nanos: 708337913
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
